<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!-- Applied to: slideshow-gen sidecar + bundled ffmpeg/ffprobe (NOT the
       Tauri shell — see app-entitlements.plist for that).

       WHY disable-library-validation:
         The PyInstaller onefile-mode sidecar extracts the PSF-signed
         Python.framework + C-extension dylibs into a temp dir at launch and
         dlopens them. Under the hardened runtime the default policy requires
         all loaded libraries to be signed by the same team as the main binary
         (Developer ID U85N54PC5J) or by Apple. The PSF-signed Python.framework
         fails that check — hence this entitlement.

         Static ffmpeg/ffprobe only dlopen Apple-signed system frameworks
         (VideoToolbox, CoreMedia, CoreVideo, AudioToolbox) which are always
         allowed regardless of this entitlement. The grant is applied here for
         uniformity; it is harmless for ffmpeg/ffprobe.

       WHY nothing else:
         - No JIT / W^X memory: Python 3.11 has no JIT; no allow-jit needed.
         - No sandbox: Marquee is direct-download; file access needs no
           security-scoped bookmark entitlement.
         - No network entitlement: the engine is fully offline.
         - Child process spawn (sidecar → ffmpeg child): allowed by default
           under the hardened runtime when the child is a properly signed binary.
           No extra entitlement is required for fork/exec.

       If the notary log names a missing entitlement, add only that one here. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
